{%- comment -%}
  "Get AED 10 off your first order" — the storefront popup.

  Usage: {% render 'welcome-popup' %} from overlays, so it is on every
  template that renders the overlay group.

  ── What it does, and what it deliberately does not ──────────────────────

  It applies the discount. /discount/CODE?redirect= is the same documented
  route the membership band and the cart strip use — see
  snippets/welcome-offer.liquid for why that is a real navigation rather than
  a background fetch. It does not print the code for the shopper to copy and
  it does not ask for an email: the code already exists and is already
  claimable, so a form here would collect an address in exchange for
  something the next click gives away anyway.

  ── Who never sees it ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Decided in Liquid wherever Liquid can decide, so the markup is absent
  rather than hidden. A customer who has ordered cannot use a first-order
  discount. A bag already carrying the code has nothing to offer. Promotions
  switched off, or the offer switched off, removes it with everything else.

  The rest is in app.js, because it depends on where the shopper is and what
  they have already dismissed, and Liquid knows neither: not over the cart,
  the checkout or the account pages — interrupting someone on their way to
  paying, or in the middle of the sign-up that was going to apply this
  anyway, is the one thing this must never do.

  ── Why the offer is not written out here ────────────────────────────────

  {% render 'welcome-offer-label' %} names it, and the minimum comes from the
  same setting the discount in admin is kept in step with. A popup is the
  loudest promise on the site; it is the last place that should be repeating
  a figure by hand.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign wp_code = settings.welcome_offer_code | default: '' | strip | upcase

  # Already on the bag: the cart-level applications carry the code as their
  # title, the same test welcome-offer makes.
  assign wp_applied = false
  for wp_d in cart.cart_level_discount_applications
    assign wp_title = wp_d.title | upcase
    if wp_title == wp_code
      assign wp_applied = true
    endif
  endfor

  assign wp_spent = false
  if customer and customer.orders_count > 0
    assign wp_spent = true
  endif

  assign wp_on = false
  if settings.welcome_popup_enabled and settings.promos_enabled and settings.welcome_offer_enabled and wp_code != blank and wp_spent == false and wp_applied == false
    assign wp_on = true
  endif

  assign wp_min = settings.welcome_offer_min | default: '' | strip
  assign wp_min_cents = 0
  if wp_min != blank
    assign wp_min_cents = wp_min | times: 100
  endif

  assign wp_back = request.path | default: '/'
-%}

{%- if wp_on -%}
  {%- capture wp_url -%}/discount/{{ wp_code | url_encode }}?redirect={{ wp_back | url_encode }}{%- endcapture -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    hidden until app.js decides, so it cannot flash on first paint — the same
    contract the review prompt uses. role="dialog" rather than alertdialog:
    nothing here is urgent, and alertdialog asks a screen reader to interrupt.
    It is not modal and does not trap focus, because it does not cover the
    page: a shopper can ignore it and carry on reading, which is the whole
    reason it sits in a corner rather than over the middle.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="wpop" id="welcomePopup" role="dialog" aria-labelledby="wpopTitle" data-wpop hidden>
    <button type="button" class="wpop-x" data-wpop-dismiss aria-label="No thanks, close this offer">
      <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">close</span>
    </button>

    <div class="wpop-body">
      <span class="mi wpop-icon" aria-hidden="true">redeem</span>
      <div class="wpop-copy">
        <p class="wpop-title" id="wpopTitle">Get {% render 'welcome-offer-label' %} off your first order</p>
        <p class="wpop-sub">
          {%- if wp_min_cents > 0 -%}
            On your first order over {{ wp_min_cents | money_with_currency }}. Applied to your bag in one tap — no code to copy.
          {%- else -%}
            Applied to your bag in one tap — no code to copy.
          {%- endif -%}
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      A real href, so it works before app.js has bound anything and for anyone
      navigating by keyboard. app.js only records the dismissal on the way
      out; it never has to intercept the click.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <a class="wpop-go" href="{{ wp_url | strip }}" data-wpop-go>
      <span class="mi" aria-hidden="true">local_offer</span>
      Apply my {% render 'welcome-offer-label' %} discount
    </a>

    <button type="button" class="wpop-no" data-wpop-dismiss>No thanks</button>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
